Pattern matching makes Java code shorter, safer, and easier to read, especially when checking an object's type and then extracting/casting its value.

1. Old Java — instanceof + casting
Object obj = "Hello";

if (obj instanceof String) {
    String str = (String) obj;
    System.out.println(str.length());
}

You had to:

Check the type.
Cast manually.
Create a variable.
2. Modern Java — Pattern Matching
Object obj = "Hello";

if (obj instanceof String str) {
    System.out.println(str.length());
}

Here:

obj instanceof String str

means:

"If obj is a String, automatically cast it and store it in str."

3. Pattern Matching with switch

Modern Java also allows pattern matching in switch:

static String process(Object obj) {

    return switch (obj) {
        case String s -> "String: " + s.length();
        case Integer i -> "Integer: " + (i * 2);
        case Double d -> "Double: " + d;
        case null -> "Null value";
        default -> "Unknown type";
    };
}

Instead of:

if (obj instanceof String) {
    ...
} else if (obj instanceof Integer) {
    ...
} else if (obj instanceof Double) {
    ...
}

The switch version is more declarative and easier to extend.

4. Record Pattern

Modern Java can also deconstruct records.

record User(String name, int age) {}

Instead of:

if (obj instanceof User user) {
    String name = user.name();
    int age = user.age();

    System.out.println(name + " " + age);
}

You can use:

if (obj instanceof User(String name, int age)) {
    System.out.println(name + " " + age);
}

It directly extracts the record components.

Why is this useful in Spring Boot?

You may encounter different request/response objects, events, exceptions, or domain types.

For example:

Object response = getResponse();

return switch (response) {
    case SuccessResponse s -> s.getData();
    case ErrorResponse e -> handleError(e);
    default -> throw new IllegalStateException("Unknown response");
};

This reduces:

Manual casting
Boilerplate
Repeated type checks
Casting-related mistakes
Nested if/else
Interview answer

Pattern matching in modern Java combines type checking, casting, and variable extraction into a single expression.
Features like pattern matching with instanceof, switch, and record patterns make code more concise, readable, and type-safe.
In Spring Boot applications, it can be useful when handling different domain objects, API responses, events, or exception types.